Pointer receivers vs Value receivers
 - In Go, methods can have either value receivers or pointer receivers.

 - A value receiver receives a copy of the struct, so modifications to the receiver's fields do not affect the original struct.
   A pointer receiver receives a copy of a pointer to the original struct, 
   allowing the method to modify the original state without copying the entire struct.

 - I prefer pointer receivers when a method needs to mutate state, when copying a large struct is undesirable,
   or when the struct contains synchronization primitives such as a mutex. Value receivers are appropriate for small, 
   value-like types when copy semantics are desirable.

 - An important distinction is method sets. A type `T` includes methods declared with value receivers,
   whereas `*T` includes methods declared with both value and pointer receivers. 
   This distinction affects interface implementation, even though Go can automatically take the address of an addressable value when calling a pointer-receiver method.

 - Finally, pointer receivers do not inherently mean heap allocation. Escape analysis determines whether a value escapes to the heap.
